# Hello World Tree — SLEIGH p-code specification.

This is the universal middle. The .slaspec file says how to turn bytes into p-code; this says what
p-code *means* once it exists, which is the half that makes the analysis portable.

## Why this file exists separately

A processor specification and the semantics of an intermediate language are different artifacts and
they change for different reasons. A new CPU means a new .slaspec. A better data-flow analysis means a
change here. Keeping them apart means adding an architecture never risks the analysis, and improving
the analysis never risks a decoder.

The naming is deliberate: `.slaspec` is the dialect, `.pspec` is the language the dialect translates
into. One of each per architecture at most, and the `.pspec` is shared by all of them.

## The instruction set of p-code

p-code is small on purpose. Fourteen operations cover every instruction on every architecture:

| Operation | Meaning | Why it is here |
|---|---|---|
| `COPY` | value to storage | the only way anything moves |
| `LOAD` | memory to register | separates address computation from the read |
| `STORE` | register to memory | the same, in reverse |
| `BRANCH` | unconditional jump | control flow as data |
| `CBRANCH` | conditional jump | the shape of every loop and every guard |
| `BRANCHIND` | computed jump | switch tables, vtables, obfuscation |
| `CALL` | transfer with return | the unit of interprocedural analysis |
| `CALLIND` | computed call | indirect dispatch |
| `RETURN` | leave a call | pairs with CALL |
| `INT` | trap into the environment | syscalls and interrupts |
| `INT_ZEXT` / `INT_SEXT` | integer widening | the source of every type confusion bug |
| `INT_ADD` `INT_SUB` `INT_XOR` `INT_AND` `INT_OR` | arithmetic and logic | the actual computation |
| `INT_MULT` `INT_DIV` `INT_REM` | multiplication and division | where overflow lives |
| `INT_EQUAL` `INT_NOTEQUAL` `INT_LESS` `INT_SLESS` `INT_LESSEQUAL` `INT_SLESSEQUAL` | comparison | produces the flags |
| `INT_ZEXT` `INT_SEXT` `INT_2COMP` `INT_NEGATE` | unary | sign handling |
| `POPCOUNT` `LZCOUNT` | bit counting | obfuscation and crypto |
| `PIECE` | concatenate two values | building wide values from parts |
| `SUBPIECE` | extract a subrange | the reverse |
| `CAST` | reinterpret bits | type punning |
| `PTRADD` `PTRSUB` | pointer arithmetic | keeps address spaces distinct from integers |

## The one rule that matters

**Every operation is typed.** A p-code value has a size in bytes and a signedness. That is not
decoration: a `LOAD` of four bytes from a pointer typed as `int *` is a different operation from a
`LOAD` of four bytes from a pointer typed as `float *`, even though the bytes are identical. The type
is what lets the analysis say "this is a float comparison" rather than "this compares two 32-bit
integers", which is the difference between a correct decompilation and a plausible wrong one.

## The two directions

The same operation table is read in both directions:

```
  bytes  --[decode]-->  p-code  --[analyse]-->  structure
  bytes  <--[encode]--  p-code  <--[synthesise]--  structure
```

Nothing in this file is direction-specific. That is what makes the mirrored engine a mirror rather
than a second implementation: the decode pass and the encode pass walk the same table and disagree
only about which end they started from.

## What a consumer needs from this layer

Four queries. Every analysis in the vessel is a composition of these:

1. **`reachable(entry)`** — which instructions can be reached from a point. Produces the basic block
   graph.
2. **`writes(register)`** — which instructions write a given register. Produces the data flow edges.
3. **`calls()`** — which instructions transfer control to a named target. Produces the call graph.
4. **`blocks()`** — basic blocks, split at every control-flow boundary. Produces the unit that every
   other pass iterates over.

Everything above — the hierarchy, the superstate grouping, the visualisation — is built from these
four and nothing else. That is the whole architecture.

## Hierarchy, and why superstates exist

A flattened basic block graph is technically complete and practically unreadable. A function with
forty blocks and no grouping is a hairball.

So the vessel groups blocks into **hierarchical states**:

```
  Superstate (the function)
    ├─ State (an if-branch, or a loop body)
    │    ├─ State (a nested guard)
    │    └─ Block
    └─ State (the else-branch)
         └─ Block
```

The rule for forming a superstate is structural, not heuristic: two states merge when they share
exactly one predecessor and one successor, which is the definition of a region with a single entry and
a single exit. That is decidable, cheap, and produces the shape a reader expects without needing to
guess at what the code was "for".

A superstate is therefore not an inference about intent. It is a statement about control flow, which
can be checked. That distinction is why this layer can be trusted when the layers above it are
heuristic.
